 "verdict": "On the full CometEls near-parabolic census (n=268), the aphelia lean toward the cap-declaration axis at d_par=+0.104 under the identical tide-aware null (p=0.0007); the spike-proxy subset (n=115) leans at +0.178 (p=0.0005).  The 206 comets absent from the 229-comet training set -- the genuinely new information -- lean at +0.071 (p=0.0309).  The census's own free dipole recovers (35,-34), 21 deg from the cap axis and 159 deg from the anti-axis -- the broadest discovered population self-organizes toward the measured sector without being told where it is.",
 "caveats": [
  "CometEls carries osculating elements at the catalogue epoch; the spike proxy uses a_osc>250 AU rather than the catalogues' original-orbit 1/a criterion -- a broader, noisier membership definition.",
  "The tide-aware null preserves |b_gal| and isotropizes galactic longitude, as in step_061; the discovery footprint is a separate confound, bounded elsewhere in the pipeline -- the geocentric observation-direction null (step_081) shows the observed-sky footprint clustering toward the ANTI-axis (R=0.73), the opposite direction from the aphelion lean measured here.",
  "Poorly determined orbits (single-apparition, parabolic-assumed) dilute any real dipole; they cannot manufacture one toward a pre-declared direction."
 ]
